592186045417] [17592186127549 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127549 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186127550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127549 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5176cfc6-698c-5192-a037-61627dd873dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127550 #edb/ref 1080 "A" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127550 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127550 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c673febf-2343-532e-b3f8-d17144d07bb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127551 #edb/ref 1080 "None of the given options is correct" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127551 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127551 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d61ae7a1-00bb-5f11-ad1d-b0434b01b6c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127552 #edb/ref 1080 "B" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127552 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127552 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e412493d-069b-51b7-a259-1b98f29e80ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127553 #edb/ref 1080 "C" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127553 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127553 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "efd3f6e9-8c99-5bdf-b917-997aad0d4a98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127554 #edb/ref 1080 "D" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127554 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127554 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "082c4fa3-468a-5f61-8c9b-166620b0d5a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128958 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5811" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128958 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following could be the graph of $y =2x^3- 6 x^2 +8?$\n\n![](images/3e/3e01f41691c4f157964157f30678f98977ac8bdf9908d89bd3e9a8817a6e2b91.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128958 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e6a96fc8-6ce5-5f47-8c20-709754ed7bc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128958 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's call $P(x)$ the given polynomial:\n\n$$\nP(x)=2x^3 -6 x^2 + 8\n$$\n\nThe degree ($n=3$) is odd and the leading coefficient ($a_3 = 2$) is positive. Therefore, the polynomial has the following end behavior:\n\n- $P(x)$ approaches $-\\infty$ as as $x$ approaches $-\\infty.$\n\n- $P(x)$ approaches $\\infty$ as $x$ approaches $\\infty.$\n\nOnly the graph $\\textbf{C}$ satisfies these conditions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128961 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5815" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128961 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/84/84c37b1692f6807873d924fad0d3591298c2b46b780797ed258dcf32d2ff0d8a.png)\n\nWhich of the following polynomials is shown in the diagram above?\n\nI. $-2 x^4 + 6 x^3 - 2 x^2 - 6 x + 4$\n\nII. $-2 x^5 + 6 x^4 + 5 x^3 - 2 x^2 - 3 x + 1$\n\nIII. $3 x^4 + 5 x^3 - 2 x^2 - 3 x - 4$\n\nIV. $- x^4 + 5 x^3 - 2 x^2 - 3 x - 1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128961 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7098a9a5-23ea-5496-a492-191c9c1a0690" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128961 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to-\\infty,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty.$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is even, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\nSo, $P(x)$ is neither II nor III.\n\nLastly, note that the $y$-intercept is $4,$ which means that $P(0)$ must be equal to $4.$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)$ is given by polynomial I." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128962 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5817" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128962 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of a polynomial $Q(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c6/c6729ee13a704ce630586cd4cbdde5c67e55c03f65f1840b2ca1f2b6c38186f2.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The coefficient of the leading term is positive.\n\nII. The degree of $Q(x)$ is odd.\n\nIII. The constant term is negative." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128962 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fdba6918-c9be-5a77-b79c-4a5cf39bf6e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128962 #edb/ref 1074 "The end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $Q(x)\\to\\infty$ as $x\\to-\\infty,$ and\n\n- $Q(x)\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $Q(x)$ is odd, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $Q(x)$ is negative.\n\nSo I is false, and II is true.\n\nLastly, note that the $y$-intercept is $0,$ which means that $Q(0)$ must be equal to $0,$ and therefore the constant term must be equal to $0.$ So, III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129142 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186561002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129142 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Graph of a Polynomial" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129142 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129142 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5dce1c69-c79d-541a-b479-8fc127352c2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129144 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129144 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129144 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129144 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129144 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129144 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921